  "place.van_south": "South of Lake Van",
  "intro.lede": "Mirror Terrain finds places on Earth that resemble target sites at the Moon's south pole and on Mars, and answers three questions: <b>where</b> to test, <b>with which combination of sites</b>, and <b>when</b>.",
  "how.t": "How to use this site",
  "how.who.t": "Who is it for?",
  "how.who": "Teams that develop landers, rovers, base hardware or drilling equipment and want to field-test them on Earth; scientists and students who study analog sites.",
  "how.what.t": "What is it for?",
  "how.what": "To see which places on Earth suit a Moon or Mars target, which sites to use together when one is not enough, in which month to go, and whether a rover would meet a difficulty there similar to the target.",
  "how.s1": "<b>Choose a target</b> on the left: a Moon or Mars site.",
  "how.s2": "<b>Choose a test purpose</b>: general, landing, rover, base or drilling. The ranking changes with the test.",
  "how.s3": "<b>Read the map.</b> Each dot is a 12 km area on Earth; lighter colours are more similar to the target. Numbered rings are the twin team. Click a dot to see its score, rock family, nearest airport and a satellite-image link.",
  "how.s4": "<b>Read the panel.</b> It shows the best match on Earth and in Türkiye, the twin team, the target and the Türkiye candidate side by side at the same scale, a feature table, the best month to test, and what the model cannot see (rock, minerals, soil, ice).",
  "how.score.t": "How to read the score:",
  "how.score": "a similarity measure from 0 to 100, where 100 means identical. Read it next to the scores of known analog sites for the same target; the panel shows this as \"% of the best known analog's score\".",
  "how.pages": "The <b>Türkiye</b> page shows the nationwide survey, <b>Rover</b> drives the same routes on the target and its Earth twins with the slope limit you choose, <b>Validation</b> shows whether the model finds known analogs, <b>Method & data</b> explains how it works and lists every source, and <b>Files</b> lets you download every table, figure and the notebook.",
  "find.t": "Key findings",
  "find.kesis": "<b>Shackleton's twin in Türkiye is {kesis}.</b> Its score is {shs}, which is {shr}% of the best known analog ({shk}, {shks}). It stays the best Türkiye window in {shrob}% of 200 random weight changes. Its twin-team window, at the same 40 m resolution: mean slope {tsl}° (Shackleton {ssl}°), area steeper than 15° {tst}% (Shackleton {sst}%).",
  "find.mer": "<b>Meridiani's best Türkiye window is {mer}</b>, at {merr}% of the best known analog's score. The model chose it without seeing minerals. NASA EMIT shows carbonate, a magnesium clay and small amounts of jarosite and hematite there, the mineral types Opportunity found at Meridiani; these matches cover a small share of pixels and are not field-verified.",
  "find.modes": "<b>Same target, different test, different place.</b> For Shackleton: landing tests at {land}; rover tests at {rover}; drilling rehearsal at {drill}.",
  "find.when": "<b>When?</b> For the Türkiye candidates the best test month is {months}: snow-free, dry and the coldest suitable month.",
  "find.limits": "<b>Limits.</b> On Mars, terrain alone does not separate analogs from controls (AUC {amat}); the barren-surface layer does (AUC {ama}). For Malapert and de Gerlache the best Türkiye window is sensitive to weights. Lake Salda, which NASA describes as a Jezero analog, scores low because the model does not see minerals.",
  "diff.t": "What makes Mirror Terrain different",
  "diff.short": "Most similarity tools answer one question: which place looks alike? Mirror Terrain answers three — where, with which combination, and when — and checks its answers against known analogs.",
  "diff.1": "<b>A twin team, not a single place.</b> No place on Earth matches a Moon or Mars target in every respect. Mirror Terrain proposes the fewest sites that together match the target, and says which feature cannot be matched on Earth at all (for Shackleton: {miss}), so that feature is tested in a lab instead.",
  "diff.2": "<b>Rankings that depend on the test.</b> Landing, rover, base and drilling tests can point to different places for the same target. For Shackleton: landing at {land}, rover at {rover}.",
  "diff.3": "<b>When to go.</b> For every candidate, the best test month (snow-free, dry, coldest) is computed from NASA climate data.",
  "diff.4": "<b>Same-resolution comparison.</b> Earth terrain is resampled to each target's own resolution (Moon 40 m, Mars 200–463 m), so differences in detail do not distort the result.",
  "diff.5": "<b>Measured validation.</b> Known analogs vs vegetated controls: AUC {am} (Moon) and {ama} (Mars). Held-out known analogs found again in the top 6%: {loo} of {n}. Weight sensitivity: 200 random trials.",
  "diff.6": "<b>A systematic survey of Türkiye.</b> All of Türkiye was scanned instead of only well-known places, giving candidates that were not described as analogs before (Keşiş Dağı, the Taurus plateau, Lake Tuz's east shore), checked against satellite images.",
  "diff.7": "<b>It shows what it cannot see.</b> Rock family, minerals (checked with NASA EMIT), soil and ice are shown next to the score without being mixed into it. Limits are stated openly, for example why Lake Salda scores low.",
  "diff.8": "<b>Open and reproducible.</b> Every result comes from open NASA, ESA, Copernicus and USGS data through one notebook. Code, data and results can be downloaded; the site is in five languages.",
  "why.t": "Why it matters",
  "why.p": "Landers, rovers and base equipment bound for the Moon and Mars are first tested on Earth, on terrain that resembles the target. Mirror Terrain makes that choice measurable:",
  "why.1": "it compares the target and Earth terrain at the <b>same resolution</b>;",
  "why.2": "it <b>validates</b> its choice by checking whether it finds known analogs;",
  "why.3": "when one place is not enough, it proposes <b>sites that together</b> match the target;",
  "why.4": "it says <b>when</b> the test should take place;",
  "why.5": "it scans Türkiye systematically and finds <b>candidates not described before</b>.",
  "mode.more": "What does this mode mean?",
  "modes.t": "Test purposes: same target, different tests",
  "modes.p": "When choosing an analog on Earth, the question is not only \"what does it look like?\" but \"what will I test?\". Mirror Terrain ranks every target five times. In each mode, the features that matter most for that test count double. The barren share keeps the same weight share in every mode, so modes change only the balance among terrain features.",
  "modes.genel": "All terrain features are balanced. It looks for the overall Earth counterpart of a target.",
  "modes.inis": "The biggest danger for a lander is a sloping or bumpy touchdown point. Mean slope, the share of area steeper than 15° and roughness count double. A distant mountain (relief) matters less for the touchdown point.",
  "modes.rover": "Rovers are stopped by the steepest slopes and rough ground. The steep-slope angle (p90), the share of area steeper than 15° and roughness count double. Landing asks \"how flat is it on average?\"; rover asks \"how hard are the hardest parts?\".",
  "modes.us": "For a base, the large-scale landform matters: crater floor, ridge or plateau. Relief, hypsometry and mean slope count double.",
  "modes.sondaj": "For drilling and sampling rehearsals, rock type matters. Terrain keeps the general weights, and windows in the same rock family as the target (volcanic, sedimentary, impact, crystalline) rank higher.",
  "modes.why.t": "Why these weights?",
  "modes.why": "They are starting values chosen from engineering reasoning, and they were tested: in every mode the validation AUC differs from the general mode by at most {md}; in drilling mode it is slightly higher (Moon {sdm}, Mars {sda}). Modes change the ranking without hurting the model's accuracy.",
  "modes.ex.t": "Example:",
  "modes.ex": "for Shackleton, landing tests point to {land}, rover tests to {rover} and drilling rehearsal to {drill}.",
